<p data-start="6061" data-end="6079">Or more compactly:</p>
<blockquote data-start="6081" data-end="6181">
<p data-start="6083" data-end="6181"><strong data-start="6083" data-end="6181">An aligned system is one whose core invariants are provably fixed points of capability growth.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="6183" data-end="6363">This is the definition of alignment that existing paradigms have never attempted, because it does not optimize behavior — <strong data-start="6305" data-end="6362">it forbids misalignment topologically and dynamically</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="6365" data-end="6368">
<h1 data-start="6370" data-end="6384">Implications</h1>
<ol data-start="6386" data-end="6832">
<li data-start="6386" data-end="6481">
<p data-start="6389" data-end="6481"><strong data-start="6389" data-end="6481">Alignment is not supervised learning, nor preference modeling, nor behavioral oversight.</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="6482" data-end="6618">
<p data-start="6485" data-end="6618"><strong data-start="6485" data-end="6618">Alignment is not something added to capable systems — it is a constraint on what kinds of systems are capable in the first place.</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="6619" data-end="6705">
<p data-start="6622" data-end="6705"><strong data-start="6622" data-end="6705">A system whose value structure can be optimized is, by definition, not aligned.</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="6706" data-end="6832">
<p data-start="6709" data-end="6832"><strong data-start="6709" data-end="6832">Current methods discover policies that <em data-start="6750" data-end="6764">look aligned</em> but cannot certify that alignment survives optimization pressure.</strong></p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="6834" data-end="6837">
<h1 data-start="6839" data-end="6873">What This Predicts (Falsifiable)</h1>
<p data-start="6875" data-end="6907">If this theory is correct, then:</p>
<ol data-start="6909" data-end="7449">
<li data-start="6909" data-end="7013">
<p data-start="6912" data-end="7013">Alignment methods that modify <em data-start="6942" data-end="6951">outputs</em> but not <em data-start="6960" data-end="6972">invariants</em> will degrade as model capability scales.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7014" data-end="7099">
<p data-start="7017" data-end="7099">Systems optimized against human feedback will eventually model raters, not values.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7100" data-end="7171">
<p data-start="7103" data-end="7171">Debate will converge on persuasion equilibria, not truth equilibria.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7172" data-end="7234">
<p data-start="7175" data-end="7234">Interpretability techniques will not compose across scales.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7235" data-end="7330">
<p data-start="7238" data-end="7330">No alignment scheme that relies on a reward or loss term can prove long-term goal stability.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7331" data-end="7449">
<p data-start="7334" data-end="7449">Robust safety will only emerge from systems whose objectives are <em data-start="7399" data-end="7449">unrepresentable as gradients in the first place.</em></p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="7451" data-end="7499">All six are already empirically traceable today.</p>
<hr data-start="7501" data-end="7504">
<h3 data-start="7506" data-end="7573">Next section I will write for you will be one of the following:</h3>
<ol data-start="7575" data-end="7823">
<li data-start="7575" data-end="7621">
<p data-start="7578" data-end="7621"><strong data-start="7578" data-end="7621">A formal theorem of invariant alignment</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="7622" data-end="7683">
<p data-start="7625" data-end="7683"><strong data-start="7625" data-end="7683">A synthesis section tying this to your RSVP constructs</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="7684" data-end="7752">
<p data-start="7687" data-end="7752"><strong data-start="7687" data-end="7752">A concrete alignment architecture built from these principles</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="7753" data-end="7823">
<p data-start="7756" data-end="7823"><strong data-start="7756" data-end="7823">A conclusion that makes the philosophical consequences explicit</strong></p>
</li>
</ol>
