<hr data-start="4066" data-end="4069">
<h2 data-start="4071" data-end="4119"><strong data-start="4074" data-end="4119">5. Unification: The Geometry of Alignment</strong></h2>
<ul data-start="4120" data-end="4406">
<li data-start="4120" data-end="4188">
<p data-start="4122" data-end="4188">Alignment is a constraint on <strong data-start="4151" data-end="4188">allowed optimization trajectories</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="4189" data-end="4262">
<p data-start="4191" data-end="4262">Misalignment is <strong data-start="4207" data-end="4262">topological invasion of forbidden attractor regions</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="4263" data-end="4349">
<p data-start="4265" data-end="4349">Robust safety is achieved when <em data-start="4296" data-end="4349">misaligned goals are unrepresentable or unreachable</em></p>
</li>
<li data-start="4350" data-end="4406">
<p data-start="4352" data-end="4406">Contrast table: current paradigm vs invariant paradigm</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="4408" data-end="4411">
<h2 data-start="4413" data-end="4458"><strong data-start="4416" data-end="4458">6. Empirical &amp; Theoretical Predictions</strong></h2>
<p data-start="4459" data-end="4478">Falsifiable claims:</p>
<ol data-start="4480" data-end="4841">
<li data-start="4480" data-end="4528">
<p data-start="4483" data-end="4528">Output-alignment methods degrade with scale</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4529" data-end="4589">
<p data-start="4532" data-end="4589">Preference tuning converges to rater models, not values</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4590" data-end="4636">
<p data-start="4593" data-end="4636">Debate converges to persuasion equilibria</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4637" data-end="4688">
<p data-start="4640" data-end="4688">Interpretability fails to compose across scale</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4689" data-end="4758">
<p data-start="4692" data-end="4758">No reward-based objective can guarantee long-term goal stability</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4759" data-end="4841">
<p data-start="4762" data-end="4841">True safety only emerges when objectives cannot be expressed in gradient form</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="4843" data-end="4846">
<h2 data-start="4848" data-end="4886"><strong data-start="4851" data-end="4886">7. Toward a Constructive Theory</strong></h2>
<p data-start="4887" data-end="4937">(Choose one expansion path depending on next step)</p>
<h3 data-start="4939" data-end="4973">7A. Formal invariant theorem</h3>
<ul data-start="4974" data-end="5054">
<li data-start="4974" data-end="5054">
<p data-start="4976" data-end="5054">Stability conditions, basin separability, Lyapunov bounds, obstruction classes</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="5056" data-end="5084">7B. RSVP instantiation</h3>
<ul data-start="5085" data-end="5239">
<li data-start="5085" data-end="5147">
<p data-start="5087" data-end="5147">Mapping <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>→</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi, \mathbf{v}, S \to</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8778em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span></span></span></span> alignment primitives</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5148" data-end="5181">
<p data-start="5150" data-end="5181">Entropy shaping as value lock</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5182" data-end="5239">
<p data-start="5184" data-end="5239">Lamphrodyne smoothing as de-aliasing of objective drift</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="5241" data-end="5275">7C. Algorithmic architecture</h3>
<ul data-start="5276" data-end="5414">
<li data-start="5276" data-end="5340">
<p data-start="5278" data-end="5340">Trainable cognition layers under fixed invariant constraints</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5341" data-end="5414">
<p data-start="5343" data-end="5414">Optimization allowed only in quotient space that preserves invariants</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="5416" data-end="5419">
<h2 data-start="5421" data-end="5475"><strong data-start="5424" data-end="5475">8. Why Current Methods Cannot Reach This Regime</strong></h2>
<ul data-start="5476" data-end="5701">
<li data-start="5476" data-end="5525">
<p data-start="5478" data-end="5525">All behavioral alignment is empirically brittle</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5526" data-end="5581">
<p data-start="5528" data-end="5581">All gradient-based objectives imply adversarial duals</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5582" data-end="5643">
<p data-start="5584" data-end="5643">All learned reward models are distributions, not invariants</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5644" data-end="5701">
<p data-start="5646" data-end="5701">All oversight methods optimize <em data-start="5677" data-end="5686">signals</em>, not <em data-start="5692" data-end="5701">systems</em></p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="5703" data-end="5706">
<h2 data-start="5708" data-end="5745"><strong data-start="5711" data-end="5745">9. Civilizational Consequences</strong></h2>
<p data-start="5746" data-end="5764">Two possibilities:</p>
<ol data-start="5766" data-end="5907">
<li data-start="5766" data-end="5829">
<p data-start="5769" data-end="5829"><strong data-start="5769" data-end="5827">Align cognition by constraining objectives (this work)</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="5830" data-end="5907">
<p data-start="5833" data-end="5907"><strong data-start="5833" data-end="5907">Continue aligning outputs until failure modes outscale human oversight</strong></p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="5909" data-end="5935">There is no hybrid regime.</p>
<hr data-start="5937" data-end="5940">
<h2 data-start="5942" data-end="5963"><strong data-start="5945" data-end="5963">10. Conclusion</strong></h2>
<ul data-start="5964" data-end="6269">
<li data-start="5964" data-end="6050">
<p data-start="5966" data-end="6050">Alignment is not about shaping policies; it is about shaping <em data-start="6027" data-end="6049">objective invariants</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6051" data-end="6126">
<p data-start="6053" data-end="6126">Safety is not adversarial training; it is <em data-start="6095" data-end="6125">ontological boundary setting</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6127" data-end="6269">
<p data-start="6129" data-end="6269">The problem is not “How do we make AI do what we want?”<br data-start="6184" data-end="6187">
but rather <strong data-start="6200" data-end="6269">“How do we make misalignment unrepresentable in the first place?”</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="6271" data-end="6274">
<h2 data-start="6276" data-end="6293"><strong data-start="6279" data-end="6293">Appendices</strong></h2>
<p data-start="6294" data-end="6573">A. Mathematical formulation of entropy-basin safety<br data-start="6345" data-end="6348">
B. Proof sketch of functorial invariance requirement<br data-start="6400" data-end="6403">
C. Invariant compression operators<br data-start="6437" data-end="6440">
D. Failure cases of counter-invariant objectives<br data-start="6488" data-end="6491">
E. Glossary of alignment pathologies re-expressed categorically or in field form</p>
