<p data-start="6982" data-end="7139">The field is attempting <strong data-start="7006" data-end="7099">control without constraint, oversight without structure, and alignment without invariants</strong>. These goals are mutually incompatible.</p>
<hr data-start="7141" data-end="7144">
<h2 data-start="7146" data-end="7185"><strong data-start="7149" data-end="7185">8.9 Core Conclusion of Section 8</strong></h2>
<blockquote data-start="7187" data-end="7366">
<p data-start="7189" data-end="7366"><strong data-start="7189" data-end="7272">Current alignment approaches ask whether a model can be trained to behave well.</strong><br data-start="7272" data-end="7275">
<strong data-start="7277" data-end="7366">Invariant alignment asks whether a model can be trained at all without behaving well.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="7368" data-end="7442">The former is a behavioral hope.<br data-start="7400" data-end="7403">
The latter is a mathematical guarantee.</p>
<hr data-start="7444" data-end="7447">
<h3 data-start="7449" data-end="7469">End of Section 8</h3>
<hr data-start="7471" data-end="7474">
<p data-start="7476" data-end="7515">If you'd like, the next section can be:</p>
