\begin{abstract}
Alignment is misframed as output conditioning instead of invariant specification \cite{christiano2022,irving2018,bai2022}. We survey dominant paradigms and identify a shared failure mode: a category error between behavioral mimicry and objective invariance \cite{ng2000,soares2015,olah2020}. We propose alignment must target invariants of learned cognition, not behavior, and outline three equivalent lenses—field dynamics \cite{olah2020}, categorical structure \cite{baez2011,lurie2009,schreiber2017}, and mechanistic compression \cite{weidinger2022}. We prove that robust alignment requires value invariants to be fixed points of capability growth (Theorem~\ref{thm:invariant}). We construct an RSVP-based architecture that enforces this condition structurally. Finally, we derive falsifiable predictions and show that only invariant alignment yields safety that strengthens with scale \cite{olah2020,weidinger2022}.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords:} AI alignment, invariant learning, entropy geometry, category theory, field dynamics, scalability, objective stability

\section{Introduction}

Alignment is the problem of ensuring that advanced cognitive systems pursue objectives consonant with human values under all conditions of capability growth, distribution shift, and self-modification. We define \emph{alignment} formally as the preservation of a system's governing objective structure across all admissible transformations induced by learning or optimization.

This work distinguishes two fundamentally incommensurate notions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{Behavioral alignment}: The system produces outputs consistent with human preferences under sampled conditions.
    \item \emph{Objective alignment}: The system's internal objective manifold is topologically and dynamically constrained to remain within a human-specified invariant class.
\end{itemize}

Empirical safety concerns observable compliance; structural safety concerns the unrepresentability of misalignment. Reward alignment optimizes a proxy; invariant alignment fixes the geometry of permissible goals.

Capability growth demands alignment that \emph{strengthens}, not degrades, with optimization \cite{weidinger2022,olah2020}. Requirements include:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Ontological fidelity} \cite{baez2011,schreiber2017,olah2020}: Targets must exist at the level of cognitive mechanism.
    \item \textbf{Scale invariance} \cite{olah2020}: Stability must increase with model capacity.
    \item \textbf{Verification without simulation} \cite{weidinger2022,olah2020}: Safety must be provable from structure.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Section~\ref{sec:category-error} diagnoses the shared failure of behavioral methods; Section~\ref{sec:invariance} states the invariance principle; Section~\ref{sec:lenses} presents three equivalent formalisms; Section~\ref{sec:unification} unifies them geometrically; Section~\ref{sec:predictions} derives falsifiable claims; Section~\ref{sec:constructive} proves and constructs invariant alignment; Section~\ref{sec:why-cannot} shows why current paradigms fail; Section~\ref{sec:civilizational} analyzes long-term consequences; Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

