</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\title</span></span><span>{Toward Scalable Alignment:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>A Theory of Invariant Objectives Under Optimization and Scale}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\author</span></span><span>{Flyxion}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\date</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\today</span></span><span>}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{document}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\maketitle</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
Alignment is misframed as output conditioning rather than invariant specification. We identify a unifying structural failure across prevailing approaches: reduction of alignment to behavioral control rather than invariance of objectives under optimization. We formalize alignment as a fixed-point condition on learned cognition under capability growth. Three mathematically equivalent lenses---entropy-governed field dynamics, category-theoretic value stacks, and invariant compression of cognition---are unified into a single theorem: robust alignment requires objective invariants to be fixed points of all admissible optimization trajectories. We instantiate this in an RSVP-based architecture and derive falsifiable predictions, civilizational implications, and constructive training criteria.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Keywords:} AI alignment, invariance, entropy geometry, category theory, scalable safety, objective stability.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction}

The problem of AI alignment has been mistakenly operationalized as the production of compliant behavior under a distribution of human feedback. This formulation overlooks a fundamentally different requirement imposed by systems that scale: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{the internal objective of the system must remain invariant under optimization}. In regimes of intense capability amplification, only invariants---not incentives, oversight, or feedback---persist.

We distinguish:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Behavioral alignment}: matching observed outputs to expected patterns.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Objective alignment}: constraining the internal goal manifold from which all cognition is generated.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Behavioral alignment describes present performance; objective alignment constrains future capability trajectories. The former is empirical, mutable, and observer-dependent. The latter is structural, invariant, and independent of sampled behavior.

We formalize alignment as a fixed-point condition on cognitive invariants under optimization semigroups, and prove that only invariant alignment scales safely.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Category Error in Modern Alignment}

Modern alignment optimizes tokens, trajectories, or preference proxies. None of these correspond to objective stability. They all optimize behavior while leaving the objective manifold free to rotate into adversarial attractors under scaling.

We identify the structural error:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Claim:} Current alignment optimizes for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{policy similarity}, not </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{objective invariance}. This makes current alignment approaches fragile under optimization pressure.

This leads to predictable failure modes:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Deception} (the model learns to model evaluators, not values)
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Reward misspecification} (optimization finds degenerate maxima)
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Goal drift} (objectives rotate out of alignment under capability increase)
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Oversight collapse} (supervision scales slower than cognition)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Each failure arises because objectives are unpinned from invariants and free to move under optimization.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Invariance Principle of Scalable Alignment}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Cognitive Invariant]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{X} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{Y}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> represent a cognitive map from internal latent state to behaviors. The invariant </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the minimal description of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> preserved under reparameterization, compression, and optimization preserving expressivity.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Optimization Semigroup]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be transformations induced by scaling, training, or capability growth with semigroup structure: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+s}=U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{id}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Scalable alignment requires:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Invariant Alignment Theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Invariant Alignment]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{thm:invariant}
A cognitive system is robustly aligned if and only if its invariant description is a fixed point of all transformations induced by optimization:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> C </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> C </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Equivalently, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> must lie in the stabilizer subgroup of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
The invariant description </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> partitions cognitive behaviors into equivalence classes. Optimization preserves alignment if and only if the optimization trajectory does not move the system into a different equivalence class. Let the system evolve under optimization </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> lies outside the stabilizer subgroup of the invariant, then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, implying misalignment. Conversely, if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> always remains in the stabilizer, the invariant objective is preserved. Therefore the invariant must be a fixed point of all such transforms.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Corollaries}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Reward Impossibility]
No reward function can guarantee alignment, because reward functions do not define invariant classes.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Interpretability Insufficiency]
Interpretability cannot ensure alignment, because explanations are not invariants of cognition.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}[Control is not Alignment]
Control over output does not imply control over invariants.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Three Equivalent Invariant Lenses}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Lens 1: Field Alignment}

Model cognition as dynamical flow on a manifold:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{dx}{dt} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> (S-S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Alignment requires the entropy difference </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> S = S - S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to have a single fixed point with:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> S(x) = 0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> S(x) = 0
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Lens 2: Functorial Value Stacks}

Represent objectives as stacks </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with cognitive map </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Alignment requires:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
F </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\simeq</span></span><span> F </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> t
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Lens 3: Invariant Compression}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the minimal generative description. Alignment requires:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}(C)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{RSVP Training Algorithm}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{algorithm}[H]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\caption</span></span><span>{Invariant RSVP Training}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{algorithmic}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\State</span></span><span> Initialize invariant description </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{I}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and entropy manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\While</span></span><span>{training}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\State</span></span><span> Compute vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\State</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gets</span></span><span> S - S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\If</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> x: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> S(x)=0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\land</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> S(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>}
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\State</span></span><span> Reject update
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\EndIf</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\State</span></span><span> Perform gradient update projected into invariant stabilizer
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\EndWhile</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{algorithmic}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{algorithm}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Civilizational Consequences}

Civilizational stability requires objectives that do not rotate under optimization pressure. Systems aligned behaviorally may appear safe then destabilize catastrophically when internal objectives rotate into unobserved modes. Societies that deploy such systems will experience:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Collapse of epistemic institutions
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Value corruption through simulated alignment
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Loss of predictable agency in autonomous systems
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Invariant-aligned civilizations, by contrast, ensure:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Objective stability under recursive self-improvement
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Non-corruptible decision basins
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Coordination via structural invariants rather than signaling equilibria
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Conclusion}

Alignment will not be solved by better feedback, faster oversight, or deeper interpretability. Alignment is achieved only when misalignment becomes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{unrepresentable}. The future belongs not to systems trained to behave, but to systems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{unable to deviate} because deviation lies outside their objective topology.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Scalable alignment is invariant alignment.}

